Chapter: ScopeRuntimeDeny
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 Scope is enforced at RUNTIME, and this is the test that says so.

 Until now a scope literal was a compile-time lint and nothing more: the
 checker refused an out-of-scope path it could SEE (CDX4002, keyed on
 the operation's name), and any path the program computed instead of
 spelling walked straight past the promise. The grant was binding on
 literals and on nothing else. Here the scope reaches the disk: the
 boot grant writes it into proc 0's process-table entry, and Fat16 asks
 for it before it touches a sector.

 This program grants itself FileSystem.Read "OK" and then does the same
 three things scope-runtime-open does with no scope at all. The
 comparison is the whole test -- same code, same blank disk, one scope
 literal apart:

   OK.TXT   is admitted by the prefix, and the write and the read-back
            both work. Enforcement that also denies the permitted case
            is just a broken filesystem.

   NO.TXT   is outside the prefix. The WRITE is refused -- and that gate
            is not on fat16-resolve-path, which is what makes it the
            interesting one: writes go through fat16-create-file and
            never touch the resolver, so a scope check placed only on
            the resolver would have let this write through while
            looking total. The read is refused too.

   the root is not admitted by a grant over "OK", so the listing is
            empty -- even though OK.TXT demonstrably exists, because we
            just wrote it and read it back. That is the third gate, and
            it is the one nobody would think to look for: neither
            fat16-list-root nor fat16-root-entries goes through the
            resolver either. A grant that cannot read a file but can
            enumerate every name on the volume has not been scoped.

 The paths here are literals only because they are easier to read. The
 fat16-* functions are ordinary functions and not effect operations, so
 the compile-time check does not key on them at all -- nothing rejected
 this program at compile time, and the refusals below are the running
 machine's.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, FileSystem.Read "OK", Device.Block] Nothing
  opening = act
    ok <- fat16-write-file "OK.TXT" "yes"
    print-line-uni ("in-scope write " & show ok)

    no <- fat16-write-file "NO.TXT" "nope"
    print-line-uni ("out-of-scope write " & show no)

    vol <- fat16-init 2048
    back <- fat16-read-text vol "OK.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("in-scope read " & maybe-text back)

    deny <- fat16-read-text vol "NO.TXT"
    print-line-uni ("out-of-scope read " & maybe-text deny)

    names <- fat16-list-root vol
    print-line-uni ("root listing " & show (list-length names))
  end

Section: Reporting

  maybe-text : Maybe Text -> Text
  maybe-text (m) = when m
    is Just (t) -> t
    is None -> "<none>"
